WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.097 --> 00:00:02.460
Arguing about the nature of the country

00:00:02.460 --> 00:00:05.330
is as American as frozen apple pie

00:00:05.330 --> 00:00:07.590
with a slice of processed cheese,

00:00:07.590 --> 00:00:10.900
says the aspirationally
acute, 80-year-old,

00:00:10.900 --> 00:00:13.020
Pulitzer Prize winner, George Will.

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.000
Now in his sixth decade as a leading voice

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.347
in debates over culture,
politics and ideas.

00:00:19.347 --> 00:00:21.077
If you don't like arguing, he says,

00:00:21.077 --> 00:00:23.320
you picked the wrong country.

00:00:23.320 --> 00:00:27.410
Will's newest book is American
Happiness and Discontents,

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:31.110
a collection of columns
called from 2008 to 2020.

00:00:31.110 --> 00:00:33.770
They cover the Great
Recession and the Obama years,

00:00:33.770 --> 00:00:37.490
to what he calls the cry-baby
presidency of Donald J. Trump,

00:00:37.490 --> 00:00:39.560
and the rise of identity politics

00:00:39.560 --> 00:00:42.640
as a major force in contemporary America.

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.290
Though he started out firmly
on the conservative right,

00:00:45.290 --> 00:00:47.510
Will has become more and more libertarian,

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:49.061
especially in his insistence

00:00:49.061 --> 00:00:51.540
that mere politics should never be

00:00:51.540 --> 00:00:54.190
the all-consuming passion
of human endeavor,

00:00:54.190 --> 00:00:56.030
and that America remains a place

00:00:56.030 --> 00:00:59.117
dedicated to a future that
is better than the present.

00:00:59.117 --> 00:01:00.930
If we can reign in our appetite

00:01:00.930 --> 00:01:03.317
for government to dispense
benefits, he says,

00:01:03.317 --> 00:01:06.300
and replace it with a government
that defends the shores,

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:10.180
fills the potholes, and
otherwise gets out of the way,

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:11.480
we're gonna see, again,

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:14.200
the creativity of the American people.

00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.280
Here is the recent
interview with George Will.

00:01:17.280 --> 00:01:19.320
George Will, thanks for talking to Reason.

00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:20.240
Glad to be with you.

00:01:20.240 --> 00:01:21.710
So, your new book,

00:01:21.710 --> 00:01:26.120
American Happiness and
Discontents: The Unruly Torrent,

00:01:26.120 --> 00:01:30.260
collects your columns from 2008 to 2020.

00:01:30.260 --> 00:01:33.700
Let's talk about this concept
of the unruly torrent.

00:01:33.700 --> 00:01:34.840
What do you mean by that?

00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:37.040
And why is that a kind
of controlling image

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.690
for this passel of, you know,

00:01:40.690 --> 00:01:43.610
interesting columns that you've collected?

00:01:43.610 --> 00:01:46.178
Well, it's unruly in the
sense that it is a torrent.

00:01:46.178 --> 00:01:51.178
That is, it is, we are, most
of reality is not governed.

00:01:51.410 --> 00:01:54.420
Most of the time that's a very good thing.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:57.650
It's been well said that
the essence of the Bible

00:01:57.650 --> 00:01:58.970
reduced to one sentence

00:01:58.970 --> 00:02:00.690
says God created man and woman

00:02:00.690 --> 00:02:02.743
and promptly lost control of events.

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:06.263
Those of us with libertarian streak,

00:02:07.150 --> 00:02:10.563
some streaks broader than
others, but mine is broad enough,

00:02:11.790 --> 00:02:14.220
believe that things being out of control

00:02:14.220 --> 00:02:16.000
is exactly what we want.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:16.833
Right.

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:18.720
We want a spontaneous order,

00:02:18.720 --> 00:02:20.470
up from the bottom creativity

00:02:20.470 --> 00:02:23.363
rather than down from the
top command structures.

00:02:24.640 --> 00:02:29.640
However, events can be unruly,
and turbulent, and dangerous,

00:02:32.950 --> 00:02:34.530
as well as constructive ways.

00:02:34.530 --> 00:02:38.590
And I think we're seeing
the dangerous side

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.330
in the last, in that
period that my book covers.

00:02:41.330 --> 00:02:44.880
Before we get to the dangerous
side and the discontent,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:49.180
let's talk about the title,
you know, American Happiness.

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.900
What, you know, what
over the past 15 years

00:02:51.900 --> 00:02:54.220
do Americans have to be happy about,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.920
that either we aren't quite celebrating

00:02:57.920 --> 00:03:01.100
or we just kind of take for granted?

00:03:01.100 --> 00:03:02.480
Well, first of all,

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.900
we're speaking shortly after
the 20th anniversary of 9/11,

00:03:05.900 --> 00:03:07.350
and we can be happy

00:03:07.350 --> 00:03:11.600
that there's been no other
mass casualty terrorist attack,

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.313
not for want of trying on
the part of the terrorist.

00:03:15.750 --> 00:03:18.570
Terrorist attacks that are preempted

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.240
or forestalled in other ways

00:03:21.430 --> 00:03:23.540
don't get noticed by definition,

00:03:23.540 --> 00:03:25.610
and therefore we tend not to credit those

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:28.400
who are in the business
of preventing them.

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:31.440
I have a son who's an FBI
Agent in Counter Terrorism

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:36.230
so I'm a little sensitive to
this non-allocation of credit.

00:03:36.230 --> 00:03:38.270
But, so there is that.

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:42.900
Second, the American economy
continues to whir along

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:46.173
in spite of the government's
best attempts to stop it.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.480
Third, we've just gone through a pandemic

00:03:50.480 --> 00:03:55.480
in which Big Pharma, the
villain du jour most days,

00:03:56.170 --> 00:04:00.110
Big Pharma stepped up
and with amazing dispatch

00:04:00.950 --> 00:04:05.867
produced several vaccines
that are safe, reliable,

00:04:07.890 --> 00:04:11.470
effective, and free essentially.

00:04:11.470 --> 00:04:13.104
So, we have that to...

00:04:13.104 --> 00:04:17.820
the basic creativity
of the Western society

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:18.790
showed itself here.

00:04:18.790 --> 00:04:21.570
Do you think there is something inherent,

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:23.890
and I, you know, I reject as you do,

00:04:23.890 --> 00:04:26.580
a kind of blood and
soil national identity,

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.110
particularly when it's related to America.

00:04:29.110 --> 00:04:31.380
But is there something in the American DNA

00:04:31.380 --> 00:04:33.990
where we cannot stay happy?

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:37.010
You know, there's that
great scene in Key Largo,

00:04:37.010 --> 00:04:39.110
the Humphrey Bogart,
Edward G. Robinson movie,

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:44.040
where at one point, Rocky,
the, you know, the bad guy

00:04:44.040 --> 00:04:46.180
played by Edward G. Robinson,

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:47.990
is asked, like, what do you want?

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:51.480
And he says, I want more, I want more.

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:54.810
Is there, you know, is there
something about America

00:04:54.810 --> 00:04:57.420
where some of the things
that you just rattled off,

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.760
you know, it's like, yeah,
okay, but what else do you have?

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:03.220
Why, you know, it seems
as if among, you know,

00:05:03.220 --> 00:05:06.580
many of our insatiable appetites

00:05:06.580 --> 00:05:09.340
is we're never very happy for very long.

00:05:09.340 --> 00:05:10.213
Yeah. That's what it's called.

00:05:10.213 --> 00:05:13.053
Someone called it the
joyless pursuit of joy.

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:16.920
I'll match your pop
culture with one of my own.

00:05:16.920 --> 00:05:18.230
And, by the way, I wanna point out

00:05:18.230 --> 00:05:23.230
that a reference to Key
Largo, pop culture is,

00:05:23.510 --> 00:05:27.216
I think that might be ancient
history by now. But, please.

